std.crypto.chacha20 — ChaCha20 stream cipher
============================================

ChaCha20 as specified in
[RFC 8439](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc8439). The core
exported function `chacha20_block` is a deterministic permutation
mapping a triple $(\text{key}, \text{counter}, \text{nonce})$ to
64 bytes of pseudorandom output. The stream cipher built on top is
just XOR of these blocks with the plaintext.

ChaCha20 is the keystream half of the ChaCha20-Poly1305 AEAD
construction in `std.crypto.aead`. It can also stand alone as a CSPRNG
generator if used carefully — but raw ChaCha20 has **no integrity** (no
MAC), so any use that an attacker can tamper with on the wire wants the
AEAD wrapper instead.

Every operation is `u32`. The whole algorithm depends on **wrapping
$\bmod\, 2^{32}$ addition** — the round function is an ARX (Add /
Rotate / Xor) design where the only nonlinearity comes from the carry
chain inside `+`.

Dependencies: none.

    module std.crypto.chacha20


### `rotl32` — 32-bit left rotation

$$
\mathrm{rotl32}(x, n) = (x \ll n) \;\lor\; (x \gg (32 - n))
$$

    rotl32(x: u32, n: u32) -> u32 = (x << n) | (x >> (32u32 - n))


### `quarter_round` — the ChaCha20 mixing core

The quarter round operates **in place** on four 32-bit words. Every
ChaCha20 round consists of eight quarter rounds — four on columns,
four on diagonals — and the full block function applies 10 double
rounds (= 20 rounds = 80 quarter rounds).

The quarter round is an ARX sequence with rotation amounts 16, 12, 8,
7. Those numbers are not arbitrary: 16 and 8 are byte-aligned and free
on most ISAs; 12 and 7 are odd, which prevents structural alignments
between successive rounds:

$$
\begin{aligned}
a \mathrel{+}= b; \quad d \mathrel{\oplus}= a; \quad d &\lll 16 \\
c \mathrel{+}= d; \quad b \mathrel{\oplus}= c; \quad b &\lll 12 \\
a \mathrel{+}= b; \quad d \mathrel{\oplus}= a; \quad d &\lll 8 \\
c \mathrel{+}= d; \quad b \mathrel{\oplus}= c; \quad b &\lll 7
\end{aligned}
$$

    quarter_round(a: *u32, b: *u32, c: *u32, d: *u32)
        *a = *a + *b
        *d = *d ^ *a
        *d = rotl32(*d, 16u32)

        *c = *c + *d
        *b = *b ^ *c
        *b = rotl32(*b, 12u32)

        *a = *a + *b
        *d = *d ^ *a
        *d = rotl32(*d, 8u32)

        *c = *c + *d
        *b = *b ^ *c
        *b = rotl32(*b, 7u32)


`chacha20_block` — generate one 64-byte keystream block
-------------------------------------------------------

`chacha20_block(key, counter, nonce, out)` writes 64 bytes of
keystream to `out`, derived from a 256-bit key (eight u32 words), a
32-bit block counter, and a 96-bit nonce (three u32 words).

### State matrix layout

The 16-word state is conceptually a 4×4 matrix laid out as:

```
cccc cccc cccc cccc    c = constant "expand 32-byte k"
kkkk kkkk kkkk kkkk    k = key words 0–3
kkkk kkkk kkkk kkkk    k = key words 4–7
bnnn nnnn nnnn nnnn    b = block counter, n = nonce
```

The four constants in row 0 spell `"expand 32-byte k"` when serialized
little-endian — the well-known DJB tag, included so that an attacker
who learns the keystream cannot reverse-engineer it into "this might
be from a different cipher".

### Algorithm

1. Initialize the 16-word state.
2. Copy state into a working buffer.
3. Apply 20 rounds (10 double rounds): each double round is four
   *column* quarter rounds followed by four *diagonal* quarter rounds.
4. Add the original state back into the working buffer (Davies–Meyer
   feedforward — the same trick that makes SHA-256 a one-way function).
5. Serialize the 16 working words little-endian into the output.

    chacha20_block(key: *u32, counter: u32, nonce: *u32, out: *byte)
        var state: [16]u32
        var working: [16]u32

The four-word constant `"expand 32-byte k"` in little-endian u32 form.

        state[0] = 0x61707865u32
        state[1] = 0x3320646eu32
        state[2] = 0x79622d32u32
        state[3] = 0x6b206574u32

Eight key words at positions 4..11.

        for i in 0..<8
            state[4 + i] = *(key + i)

Block counter at position 12; three nonce words at 13..15.

        state[12] = counter
        state[13] = *(nonce + 0)
        state[14] = *(nonce + 1)
        state[15] = *(nonce + 2)

Snapshot the state — the post-round result will be added back to it.

        for i in 0..<16
            working[i] = state[i]

Twenty rounds = ten double rounds. Each double round is four column
quarter rounds followed by four diagonal quarter rounds.

        for r in 0..<10
            quarter_round(working,        &working[4], &working[8],  &working[12])
            quarter_round(&working[1], &working[5], &working[9],  &working[13])
            quarter_round(&working[2], &working[6], &working[10], &working[14])
            quarter_round(&working[3], &working[7], &working[11], &working[15])

            quarter_round(working,        &working[5], &working[10], &working[15])
            quarter_round(&working[1], &working[6], &working[11], &working[12])
            quarter_round(&working[2], &working[7], &working[8],  &working[13])
            quarter_round(&working[3], &working[4], &working[9],  &working[14])

Davies–Meyer feedforward: add the original state into the round output.
This is what makes the function one-way — without it, the inverse round
function could simply reverse the 20 rounds.

        for i in 0..<16
            working[i] = working[i] + state[i]

Serialize each u32 little-endian into 4 output bytes (16 words × 4 = 64).

        for i in 0..<16
            val v = working[i]
            *(out + i * 4)     = byte(v)
            *(out + i * 4 + 1) = byte(v >> 8)
            *(out + i * 4 + 2) = byte(v >> 16)
            *(out + i * 4 + 3) = byte(v >> 24)


Tests
=====

Tests verify against the published RFC 8439 reference vectors. The
canonical test key is bytes `0x00..0x1f`, which packs little-endian
into the u32 words `0x03020100, 0x07060504, …, 0x1f1e1d1c`. The
canonical test nonce is `00 00 00 09 00 00 00 4a 00 00 00 00`, which
packs LE into `0x09000000, 0x4a000000, 0x00000000`. The block counter
is 1 in the longer worked example.

### `quarter_round` — RFC 8439 §2.1.1 reference vector

A single quarter round on the four published input words must produce
the four published output words.

    #test
    test_quarter_round() -> unit
        var a: u32 = 0x11111111u32
        var b: u32 = 0x01020304u32
        var c: u32 = 0x9b8d6f43u32
        var d: u32 = 0x01234567u32

        quarter_round(&a, &b, &c, &d)

        assert(a == 0xea2a92f4u32, "a")
        assert(b == 0xcb1cf8ceu32, "b")
        assert(c == 0x4581472eu32, "c")
        assert(d == 0x5881c4bbu32, "d")


### `chacha20_block` — RFC 8439 Appendix A.1 Test Vector #1

All-zero key, all-zero nonce, counter 0. The first 16 output bytes
must be `76 b8 e0 ad a0 f1 3d 90 40 5d 6a e5 53 86 bd 28`.

    #test
    test_chacha20_block_rfc7539() -> unit
        var key: [8]u32

        for i in 0..<8
            key[i] = 0u32

        var nonce: [3]u32
        nonce[0] = 0u32
        nonce[1] = 0u32
        nonce[2] = 0u32

        var out: [64]byte

        chacha20_block(key, 0u32, nonce, out)

        val expected: [16]byte = [0x76, 0xb8, 0xe0, 0xad, 0xa0, 0xf1, 0x3d, 0x90, 0x40, 0x5d, 0x6a, 0xe5, 0x53, 0x86, 0xbd, 0x28]

        for i in 0..<16
            assert(out[i] == expected[i], s"byte $i: got ${int(out[i])} want ${int(expected[i])}")


### Determinism — same inputs produce identical output

A pure block function over a fixed `(key, counter, nonce)` must be
bitwise reproducible across calls.

    #test
    test_chacha20_deterministic() -> unit
        var key: [8]u32
        key[0] = 0xdeadbeefu32
        key[1] = 0xcafebabeu32
        key[2] = 0x12345678u32
        key[3] = 0x9abcdef0u32
        key[4] = 0x11111111u32
        key[5] = 0x22222222u32
        key[6] = 0x33333333u32
        key[7] = 0x44444444u32

        var nonce: [3]u32
        nonce[0] = 0x00000001u32
        nonce[1] = 0x00000002u32
        nonce[2] = 0x00000003u32

        var out1: [64]byte
        var out2: [64]byte

        chacha20_block(key, 0u32, nonce, out1)
        chacha20_block(key, 0u32, nonce, out2)

        for i in 0..<64
            assert(out1[i] == out2[i], "deterministic")


### Different counters → different output

If incrementing the block counter did not change the keystream, the
cipher would output the same 64 bytes for every block — utterly
broken. The test asserts that *some* byte differs; it cannot prove
distinguishability strength, only that the counter is wired up.

    #test
    test_chacha20_different_counters() -> unit
        var key: [8]u32

        for i in 0..<8
            key[i] = u32(i)

        var nonce: [3]u32
        nonce[0] = 0u32
        nonce[1] = 0u32
        nonce[2] = 0u32

        var out0: [64]byte
        var out1: [64]byte

        chacha20_block(key, 0u32, nonce, out0)
        chacha20_block(key, 1u32, nonce, out1)

        var differs = false

        for i in 0..<64
            if out0[i] != out1[i] then differs = true

        assert(differs, "different counters produce different output")


Design notes
------------

**u32 wrapping is the entire round function.** Every `+` in the
quarter round is $\bmod\, 2^{32}$ addition. The language guarantees
this — Sysl's `u32` arithmetic wraps without trapping — and any change
to that semantic (e.g. inserting overflow checks) would break this
file.

**No allocation.** The block function uses only stack-allocated fixed
arrays. No heap, no refcounting, no GC pressure. This matters: a
primitive that may be called millions of times per second needs to be
allocator-free, and ChaCha20 is the keystream generator under both the
AEAD and any future CSPRNG wrapper.

**No Poly1305 inside this module.** ChaCha20 alone provides
confidentiality but not integrity — a single bit-flip in the
ciphertext silently produces garbage plaintext with no detection.
Pair with Poly1305 (`std.crypto.aead`) for authenticated encryption.
Using bare ChaCha20 over an attacker-controlled channel is a
security bug in most contexts.

**CSPRNG wrapper deferred.** A `ChaChaRng` interface needs a key
management story (raw key in? SHA-256 seeding? `/dev/urandom`
bootstrap?) that has not been pinned down yet. The block function is
the foundation; the random-stream interface layers on top once that
story exists.
